/**
 * @module ast
 *
 * The {Node} type that `zuri.parse()` returns, which is a generic
 * representation of one node of the syntax tree. It's tagged with
 * `kind`, such as `'Binary'`, `'If'` or `'Function'`, and has a
 * `fields` dictionary with whatever that kind of node contains. There's
 * a single `Node` class, not one class per grammar rule, because the
 * grammar has around fifty kinds of expression, statement and
 * declaration, and a class for each would mean fifty almost identical
 * classes to keep in step with the parser. A `fields` entry that's
 * itself a node, or a list of nodes, is already wrapped in a {Node}, so
 * you never need to call `Node.from_dict()` yourself.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * for node in zuri.parse('def add(a, b) { return a + b }') {
 *   echo node.kind
 * }
 * # Function
 * ```
 *
 * The tree is the program as it was written. A `for` loop is a `For`
 * node, `x += 1` is a `CompoundAssign`, and an interpolated string is
 * an `Interpolation`. None of them is rewritten into the simpler forms
 * that the compiler turns them into.
 *
 * ## Positions
 *
 * Every node records the part of the source it was read from:
 *
 * | Attribute | Meaning |
 * | --- | --- |
 * | `start` | The offset of its first character |
 * | `end` | The offset just past its last character |
 * | `line`, `col` | Where it starts, both counted from 1 |
 * | `end_line`, `end_col` | Where it ends: the column just past its last character |
 *
 * Offsets count characters, not bytes, and index the source the way
 * slicing does, so `source[node.start, node.end]` is exactly the node's
 * own text. A node covers its delimiters: a string literal's quotes, a
 * block's braces, a call's parentheses.
 *
 * A statement or declaration starts at its keyword: `var total = 1`
 * starts at `var` and `def add(a, b)` at `def`. A class member starts
 * at its first word, `static` included. In a list of declarations,
 * `var a = 1, b`, each `Var` spans from its name to the end of its
 * value.
 *
 * A node that names something through a name of its own also says where
 * that name is, in its `fields`:
 *
 * | Kind | Field | Covers |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | `Function`, `Method`, `Class` | `name_span` | The declared name |
 * | `Property`, `Var`, `Argument` | `name_span` | The declared name |
 * | `Get`, `Set` | `name_span` | The property name after the `.` |
 * | `For` | `key_span`, `value_span` | Each loop variable |
 * | `Import` | `segments` | Each part of the path |
 *
 * A span field is a plain dict with the same six keys a node has, and a
 * `For` without a key has a `key_span` of `nil`. An import segment adds
 * `text`, the part as written: `'.'`, `'..'` or a name.
 *
 * A node the source leaves implicit has an empty span, `start == end`,
 * where it would have been written: the `nil` of a `return` with no
 * value or of a `var` with no initializer sits just after the keyword
 * or name, and so does the `Any` type of an untyped parameter and the
 * `List` type of a `...` parameter.
 *
 * ## Comments and doc blocks
 *
 * Every `#` comment and doc block is returned as a node of its own, of
 * kind `'Comment'` or `'DocBlock'`, exactly where it appeared: as a
 * sibling in the surrounding list of statements or declarations, at the
 * top level, inside a `{ }` block, or between two class members.
 *
 * A comment written in the middle of a statement, such as among a
 * call's arguments or between an `if` block's `}` and its `else`, has
 * no place among that statement's own nodes. It's returned as a sibling
 * placed just after the smallest statement, declaration or class member
 * that contains it, at that statement's depth. A comment inside an `if`
 * body three blocks deep is returned three blocks deep, never moved up
 * to the top level. Its position still says where it was written.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * var source = "def foo() {\n  echo bar(a, # x\n  b)\n  echo 2\n}\n"
 * var body = zuri.parse(source)[0].fields.body.fields.statements
 * echo body[0].kind        # Echo, the statement the comment sat inside
 * echo body[1].kind        # Comment, placed right after it
 * echo body[1].fields.text # ' x'
 * echo body[2].kind        # Echo, the "echo 2" line, unaffected
 * ```
 *
 * A comment above a declaration is returned just before it:
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * var source = "# a helper function\ndef add(a, b) {\n  return a + b\n}\n"
 * var nodes = zuri.parse(source)
 * echo nodes[0].kind        # Comment
 * echo nodes[0].fields.text # " a helper function"
 * echo nodes[1].kind        # Function
 * ```
 *
 * A class body's fields and methods are two separate lists, a `Class`
 * node's `properties` and `methods`, so a comment between a field and a
 * method lands in `methods`, the list of the member right after it, and
 * a comment between two fields lands in `properties`. A comment after
 * the last member goes to the list of the member before it. To rebuild
 * a class body in its written order, merge the two lists and sort them
 * by `start`.
 *
 * ## Node kinds
 *
 * Expressions:
 *
 * | Kind | Fields |
 * | --- | --- |
 * | `Nil`, `Self`, `Parent` | None |
 * | `Bool`, `Integer`, `Float`, `BigNumber` | `value` |
 * | `Literal` | `value`: a string's text, or a dictionary key written as a name |
 * | `Interpolation` | `parts`: a string for each run of text and a node for each `${...}` |
 * | `Identifier` | `name` |
 * | `Unary` | `op`, `operand` |
 * | `Binary` | `left`, `op`, `right`, for the arithmetic, bitwise and shift operators |
 * | `Logical` | `left`, `op`, `right`, for the comparisons |
 * | `Circuit` | `left`, `op`, `right`, for `and` and `or` |
 * | `Condition` | `condition`, `then`, `otherwise`: `a ? b : c` |
 * | `Grouping` | `inner`: an expression in parentheses |
 * | `Range` | `lower`, `upper` |
 * | `Call` | `callee`, `arguments` |
 * | `Get` | `object`, `name`, `name_span` |
 * | `Set` | `object`, `name`, `name_span`, `value`: `object.name = value` |
 * | `Index` | `object`, `index` |
 * | `Slice` | `object`, `lower`, `upper`; an omitted bound is an implicit `Nil` |
 * | `List` | `items` |
 * | `Dict` | `keys`, `values`, in matching order |
 * | `Shorthand` | `name`: the value of a `{ name }` entry, which reads `name` |
 * | `Assign` | `target`, `value`: `target = value` |
 * | `CompoundAssign` | `target`, `op`, `value`: `target += value` and its kin |
 * | `Update` | `target`, `op`: `target++` or `target--` |
 * | `Anonymous` | `declaration`: a `Function` named `@anon` and a number |
 * | `TypeHint` | `types`, `nullable` |
 * | `Argument` | `name`, `name_span`, `type_hint` |
 *
 * A `Literal`'s `value` leaves out the quotes, and an `Interpolation`'s
 * text parts come with their escapes already applied. A
 * `CompoundAssign`'s `op` and an `Update`'s name the operator written,
 * such as `'PlusEq'` or `'Increment'`.
 *
 * Each entry of a `TypeHint`'s `types` is a node named after the type:
 * `Any`, `Bool`, `Int`, `Number`, `BigInt`, `String`, `Bytes`, `List`,
 * `Dict`, `Range`, `File`, `Function`, `Type`, `Callable`, `Iterable`,
 * or `Instance` for a class. Every one carries `class_name`, the class
 * an `Instance` names and `nil` on the rest.
 *
 * Statements:
 *
 * | Kind | Fields |
 * | --- | --- |
 * | `Expression`, `Echo`, `Raise`, `Return` | `value` |
 * | `Var` | `name`, `name_span`, `value`, `type_hint`, `is_constant` |
 * | `VarList` | `declarations`: the `Var` nodes of `var a = 1, b = 2` |
 * | `Block` | `statements` |
 * | `If` | `condition`, `then`, `otherwise` |
 * | `While` | `condition`, `body` |
 * | `DoWhile` | `body`, `condition` |
 * | `For` | `key`, `key_span`, `value`, `value_span`, `iterable`, `body` |
 * | `Iter` | `initializer`, `condition`, `steps`, `body` |
 * | `Using` | `subject`, `arms`, `default_body` |
 * | `When` | `labels`, `body`: one arm of a `Using` |
 * | `Catch` | `body`, `catch_body`, `error_var` |
 * | `Assert` | `condition`, `message` |
 * | `Import` | `path`, `segments`, `name`, `aliased`, `elements`, `imports_all`, `exported` |
 * | `Continue`, `Break` | None |
 * | `Decl` | `declaration`: a function declared inside a block |
 * | `Comment`, `DocBlock` | `text`: the comment without its `#`, `/*` and `*/` |
 *
 * A `For` written as `for value in iterable` has a `key` of `nil`. An
 * `Iter` clause left empty is `nil`, and with no steps `steps` is an
 * empty list.
 *
 * Declarations:
 *
 * | Kind | Fields |
 * | --- | --- |
 * | `Stmt` | `statement`: a statement at the top level |
 * | `Function` | `name`, `name_span`, `parameters`, `body`, `is_variadic` |
 * | `Class` | `name`, `name_span`, `superclass`, `properties`, `methods`, `is_extension` |
 * | `Property` | `name`, `name_span`, `value`, `type_hint`, `is_static`, `is_constant` |
 * | `Method` | `name`, `name_span`, `parameters`, `body`, `is_variadic`, `is_static` |
 *
 * An `Import`'s `name` is the `Identifier` it binds: the name after
 * `as` when `aliased` is true, otherwise the last part of its path,
 * placed on that part. `path` joins every part with the platform's path
 * separator. A decorated method's `name` keeps its `@`, as in `'@new'`.
 *
 * @copyright 2026, Richard Ore and Zuri contributors
 * === libs/zuri/compile.zu
 * @module compile
 *
 * The {Instr} type `zuri.compile()` returns: a generic representation
 * of one VM instruction, tagged by `op` (`'LoadConst'`, `'Add'`,
 * `'Jmp'`, ...) with a `fields` dict holding that opcode's own
 * operands. As with {zuri.ast.Node}, there's one `Instr` class rather
 * than one class per opcode. The instruction set has around sixty
 * opcodes, and a dedicated class per opcode would be sixty
 * near-identical classes to keep in sync with the compiler forever
 * after.
 */

import _zuri_parse
import ._tree
import .diagnostic { Diagnostic }

/**
 * One AST node: an expression, statement, declaration, type
 * hint, or a standalone comment/doc block sitting between two of
 * those.
 *
 * @printable
 */
class Node {

  /**
   * Creates a {Node} directly from field values that are already
   * wrapped. Ordinary Zuri code should use `Node.from_dict()` or
   * `zuri.parse()` instead, because this constructor doesn't wrap
   * anything itself.
   *
   * @param string kind: the node's kind, such as `'Binary'` or `'If'`
   * @param dict position: where the node is in its source, as
   *    `{ start, end, line, col, end_line, end_col }`. See this
   *    module's documentation for what each one counts.
   * @param dict fields: this kind's own fields, already wrapped
   */
  @new(kind: string, position: dict, fields: dict) {
    self.kind = kind
    self.start = position.start
    self.end = position.end
    self.line = position.line
    self.col = position.col
    self.end_line = position.end_line
    self.end_col = position.end_col
    self.fields = fields
  }

  /**
   * Builds a {Node} from a dict holding a node's parts (`{ kind,
   * start, end, line, col, end_line, end_col, fields }`),
   * recursively wrapping any node-shaped entry inside `fields`, a
   * single nested node or a list of them, into its own {Node}. A `fields` entry that isn't
   * node-shaped (a string, a number, a bool, `nil`, or a plain
   * dict such as a `name_span`) passes through unchanged.
   *
   * @param dict data: `{ kind, start, end, line, col, end_line,
   *    end_col, fields }`
   * @returns Node
   */
  static from_dict(data: dict) {
    var fields = {}
    for key in data.fields.keys() {
      fields[key] = _wrap_node(data.fields[key])
    }
    return Node(data.kind, data, fields)
  }

  /**
   * Is this node a standalone comment or doc block (as opposed
   * to a real expression/statement/declaration)? See this
   * module's own doc comment for exactly where these can and
   * can't appear.
   *
   * @returns bool
   */
  is_trivia() {
    return self.kind == 'Comment' or self.kind == 'DocBlock'
  }

  /**
   * This node as `kind@line:col`, such as `'Binary@3:5'`, giving the
   * line and column where it starts. It's only a one-line summary. Use
   * `dump()` to see what's inside a node.
   *
   * @returns string
   */
  to_string() {
    return _header(self)
  }

  @to_string() {
    return self.to_string()
  }

  /**
   * A readable, indented dump of this node and everything inside it,
   * over several lines. This is the way to find out what a `fields`
   * entry is called for a kind you haven't looked up yet: run
   * `echo node.dump()` and read it, instead of guessing a key and
   * checking whether it exists.
   *
   * ```zuri
   * import zuri
   *
   * var nodes = zuri.parse('def add(a, b) { return a + b }')
   * echo nodes[0].dump()
   * ```
   *
   * prints
   *
   * ```
   * Function@1:1
   *   name: 'add'
   *   name_span: 1:5-1:8
   *   parameters:
   *     Argument@1:9
   *       name: 'a'
   *       name_span: 1:9-1:10
   *       type_hint:
   *         TypeHint@1:10
   *           types:
   *             Any@1:10
   *               class_name: nil
   *           nullable: true
   *     Argument@1:12
   *       name: 'b'
   *       name_span: 1:12-1:13
   *       type_hint:
   *         TypeHint@1:13
   *           types:
   *             Any@1:13
   *               class_name: nil
   *           nullable: true
   *   body:
   *     Block@1:15
   *       statements:
   *         Return@1:17
   *           value:
   *             Binary@1:24
   *               left:
   *                 Identifier@1:24
   *                   name: 'a'
   *               op: 'Plus'
   *               right:
   *                 Identifier@1:28
   *                   name: 'b'
   *   is_variadic: false
   * ```
   *
   * Each node's heading is its kind, and the line and column where it
   * starts. A span field such as `name_span` is shown as the range it
   * covers, `line:col-end_line:end_col`. The parameters without types
   * have an implicit `Any` type just after their names, and
   * `class_name` is `nil` for `Any`, because only an `Instance` type
   * names a class.
   *
   * @returns string
   */
  dump() {
    return "\n".join(_dump_lines(self, 0))
  }
}

/**
 * Recursively wraps a raw value from a node's `fields` dict: a
 * node-shaped dict becomes a {Node}, a list has this applied to
 * each of its own entries, and everything else (a string,
 * number, bool, `nil`, or a plain non-node dict) passes through
 * as-is.
 *
 * A dict counts as "node-shaped" when it carries both a `kind`
 * and a `fields` entry, the two keys nothing else `zuri.parse()`
 * produces happens to have together, so this can't misfire on a
 * `TypeHint`'s plain `{ types, nullable }` fields dict or
 * similar.
 */
def _wrap_node(value) {
  if is_list(value) {
    var result = []
    for item in value {
      result.append(_wrap_node(item))
    }
    return result
  }

  if is_dict(value) and
      value.get('kind', nil) != nil and
      value.get('fields', nil) != nil {
    return Node.from_dict(value)
  }

  return value
}

# --- dump() support ---
#
# Builds a list of already-indented lines rather than one long string,
# so a nested call can just `.extend()` the caller's own list instead
# of juggling partial-line concatenation.

def _indent(level) {
  var s = ''
  iter var i = 0; i < level; i++ {
    s = s + '  '
  }
  return s
}

def _dump_scalar(value) {
  if value == nil {
    return 'nil'
  }
  if is_string(value) {
    return "'" + value + "'"
  }
  if _is_span(value) {
    var range = '${value.line}:${value.col}-${value.end_line}:${value.end_col}'

    if value.contains('text') {
      return "'${value.text}'@${range}"
    }

    return range
  }
  return '${value}'
}

# A `name_span`, a `key_span`, a `value_span` or an import segment: a
# plain dict holding a position.
def _is_span(value) {
  return is_dict(value) and
      value.contains('start') and
      value.contains('end_col')
}

# Shared by `Node.to_string()` and `dump()`'s own node header line.
def _header(node) {
  return '${node.kind}@${node.line}:${node.col}'
}

def _dump_lines(value, indent) {
  if instance_of(value, Node) {
    var lines = [_indent(indent) + _header(value)]
    for key in value.fields.keys() {
      var field_value = value.fields[key]
      if instance_of(field_value, Node) or is_list(field_value) {
        lines.append(_indent(indent + 1) + key + ':')
        lines.extend(_dump_lines(field_value, indent + 2))
      } else {
        lines.append(
          _indent(indent + 1) + key + ': ' + _dump_scalar(field_value),
        )
      }
    }
    return lines
  }

  if is_list(value) {
    if value.is_empty() {
      return [_indent(indent) + '[]']
    }
    var lines = []
    for item in value {
      lines.extend(_dump_lines(item, indent))
    }
    return lines
  }

  return [_indent(indent) + _dump_scalar(value)]
}

/**
 * Parses all of `source`, and returns its top-level declarations as a
 * list of {Node} objects. See this module's documentation for what they
 * look like, and in particular for what's kept around comments.
 *
 * If there's a syntax error, this raises an error, instead of returning
 * a partial result, because there's no meaningful syntax tree to return
 * for source that the parser couldn't understand.
 *
 * @param string source: the Zuri source to parse
 * @returns list[Node]
 * @throws Error if `source` has a syntax error
 */
def parse(source) {
  return _zuri_parse.parse(source, Node)
}

/**
 * What `parse_partial()` read from a piece of source: the tree it
 * built and every syntax error it found on the way.
 *
 * @printable
 */
class ParseResult {

  /**
   * Returns a new {ParseResult}. Ordinary code gets these from
   * `parse_partial()`, instead of creating them.
   *
   * @param list nodes: the top-level nodes that were read, as `parse()`
   *    returns them
   * @param list errors: every syntax error, as {zuri.Diagnostic}
   *    objects, in the order they occur in the source
   */
  @new(nodes: list, errors: list) {
    self.nodes = nodes
    self.errors = errors
  }

  /**
   * True if the source parsed without any errors, in which case `nodes`
   * is the same as what `parse()` returns.
   *
   * @returns bool
   */
  is_clean() {
    return self.errors.is_empty()
  }

  /**
   * This result as `ParseResult(n nodes, m errors)`.
   *
   * @returns string
   */
  to_string() {
    return 'ParseResult(${self.nodes.length()} nodes, ${self.errors.length()} errors)'
  }

  @to_string() {
    return self.to_string()
  }
}

/**
 * Parses `source` as far as it can and returns everything it read
 * along with every syntax error it found, without raising.
 *
 * At a statement it cannot read, the parser reports the problem,
 * skips to where the next statement starts and carries on, so one
 * mistake is reported once and the rest of the source still comes
 * back. The statement that failed comes back as far as it was
 * read, or as a `None` node when nothing of it could be kept. A
 * name the source is missing, such as the variable name in `var =
 * 2`, comes back as an empty string with an empty span where the
 * name belongs.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * var result = zuri.parse_partial('var a = 1\nvar = 2\nvar c = 3')
 *
 * echo result.errors[0]       # 2:5: Variable name expected.
 * echo result.nodes.length()  # 3
 * ```
 *
 * @param string source: The Zuri source to parse
 * @returns ParseResult
 */
def parse_partial(source: string) {
  var raw = _zuri_parse.parse_partial(source, Node)
  var errors = raw.errors.map(@(error) => Diagnostic(error.message, error))

  return ParseResult(raw.nodes, errors)
}

/**
 * Reads the file at `path`, and parses as much of it as it can. This is
 * the same as `parse_partial(file(path).read())`.
 *
 * @param string path: the path of the Zuri source file to parse
 * @returns ParseResult
 * @throws Error if `path` can't be opened or read
 */
def parse_partial_file(path: string) {
  return parse_partial(file(path).read())
}

/**
 * Reads the file at `path`, and parses its contents. This is the same
 * as `parse(file(path).read())`, for the common case of parsing a file
 * instead of a string of source.
 *
 * @param string path: the path of the Zuri source file to parse
 * @returns list[Node]
 * @throws Error if `path` can't be opened or read, or has a syntax
 *    error
 */
def parse_file(path) {
  return parse(file(path).read())
}

/**
 * Reads, parses, and dumps the file at `path` in one call: the
 * fastest way to answer "what does this file's AST actually look
 * like". Equivalent to parsing `path` and joining every
 * resulting top-level node's own `dump()` with a blank line
 * between them.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 * echo zuri.dump_file('main.zu')
 * ```
 *
 * @param string path: Path to the Zuri source file to parse and
 *    dump
 * @returns string
 * @throws Error if `path` can't be opened or read, or has a
 *    syntax error
 */
def dump_file(path) {
  var dumps = []
  for node in parse_file(path) {
    dumps.append(node.dump())
  }
  return "\n\n".join(dumps)
}

def _describe(value) {
  if instance_of(value, Node) {
    return value.kind
  }
  return nil
}

/**
 * Visits every node that can be reached from `nodes`, which is usually
 * the result of `zuri.parse()` or a single {Node} taken from it, in the
 * order they appear, and calls `visitor` for each one. This is the tool
 * for doing something with every `X`. Most real uses of a syntax tree
 * are walks, not navigation from field to field.
 *
 * `visitor` is either a plain function, called with every node as
 * `visitor(node)`, or a dictionary that maps kinds to functions, so
 * that only nodes of those kinds are visited, as
 * `visitor[node.kind](node)`. Use the dictionary form when you only
 * care about one or two kinds, because it reads like a small dispatch
 * table, instead of a chain of `if node.kind == ...` checks inside one
 * function.
 *
 * The whole tree is always visited, because `visitor` can't choose to
 * skip a node's children.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * var nodes = zuri.parse(source)
 *
 * # The name of every function declared in the file, including nested
 * # functions.
 * var names = []
 * zuri.walk_nodes(nodes, {
 *   Function: @(node) { names.append(node.fields.name) },
 * })
 * ```
 *
 * @param Node|list[Node] nodes
 * @param function|dict visitor
 */
def walk_nodes(nodes, visitor) {
  _tree.walk(nodes, _describe, visitor, is_dict(visitor))
}

/**
 * Every node reachable from `nodes` whose `kind` is `kind`, in
 * the order they appear. Shorthand for the common "just give me
 * a flat list of these" case `walk_nodes()` itself is built on.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * var nodes = zuri.parse(source)
 * for comment in zuri.find_nodes(nodes, 'Comment') {
 *   echo comment.fields.text
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * @param Node|list[Node] nodes
 * @param string kind
 * @returns list[Node]
 */
def find_nodes(nodes, kind) {
  return _tree.find(nodes, _describe, kind)
}
